2. Set
Objective

Use a Set<Id> from a SOQL query so duplicate IDs are automatically removed.

Code
Set<Id> memberIds = new Set<Id>();

for(Member__c member : [
    SELECT Id
    FROM Member__c
]) {
    memberIds.add(member.Id);
}

System.debug('Number of unique Members: ' + memberIds.size());
Concept

A Set stores unique values.

List → Allows duplicates
Set  → Does not allow duplicates
Expected Output
Number of unique Members: <number>

The exact number depends on the records currently present in your Salesforce org. The PDF says the Set size should equal the number of distinct records in the org.

3. Map
Objective

Create a Map<Id, Member__c> from SOQL and retrieve a record using its Id.

Code
Map<Id, Member__c> memberMap = new Map<Id, Member__c>(
    [SELECT Id, Name FROM Member__c]
);

for(Id memberId : memberMap.keySet()) {
    Member__c member = memberMap.get(memberId);

    System.debug(
        'Member Id: ' + member.Id +
        ' | Member Name: ' + member.Name
    );

    break;
}
Concept

A Map stores data in key-value pairs.

Key       → Id
Value     → Member record

The PDF specifically asks for a Map lookup that returns the correct field value rather than null or an error.

4. Governor Limits & Bulkification
Objective

Understand why Salesforce code must be bulkified.

The PDF identifies bulkification as the most important section of Day 2 and asks us to deliberately create a bad trigger and then fix it.

5. Bad Trigger — SOQL Inside Loop
Code
trigger MemberBadTrigger on Member__c (after insert) {

    for(Member__c member : Trigger.new) {

        List<Member__c> members = [
            SELECT Id, Name
            FROM Member__c
            WHERE Id = :member.Id
        ];

        System.debug(members);
    }
}
Why this is bad

The SOQL query is executed inside the loop.

If 200 records are inserted:

200 records
   ↓
Loop runs 200 times
   ↓
SOQL runs 200 times
   ↓
Governor Limit exceeded

Salesforce has governor limits, so this design is not production-safe.

The PDF asks you to insert around 200+ records and capture the governor-limit failure.

Screenshot

Capture:

Governor Limit Error

The PDF gives "Too many SOQL queries: 101" as the example failure to look for.

6. Fixed / Bulkified Version

Instead of querying inside the loop, collect IDs first and perform SOQL once.

Code
trigger MemberBulkTrigger on Member__c (after insert) {

    Set<Id> memberIds = new Set<Id>();

    for(Member__c member : Trigger.new) {
        memberIds.add(member.Id);
    }

    List<Member__c> members = [
        SELECT Id, Name
        FROM Member__c
        WHERE Id IN :memberIds
    ];

    for(Member__c member : members) {
        System.debug('Member Name: ' + member.Name);
    }
}
Bulkified Flow
Trigger.new
     ↓
Collect IDs into Set
     ↓
ONE SOQL query
     ↓
Process records
Expected Result

200+ records should process without the governor-limit error. The PDF explicitly asks for a screenshot showing the successful fixed version.

Screenshots

